$\\mathsf{polylog}(N)$ proof size and verification. We leverage this PCS to obtain a lookup argument for \\textit{static} tables of size $N$ and $k\\ll N$ lookups with $k\\cdot\\mathsf{polylog}(N)$ online prover time and $\\mathsf{polylog}(N)$ verification and proof size. We further extend the approach to \\textit{updatable} tables, obtaining $O(k(N^\\epsilon+\\mathsf{polylog}(N)))$ amortized prover time for a $k$-sized batch of reads and writes, where $\\epsilon \\in (0,1)$. We retain $\\mathsf{polylog}(N)$ proof size and verification. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBatch, Pack, and Prove (https://fhe.org/conferences/conference-2026/resources/slides/1440_Guimaraes.pdf)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; We need a  (multivariate) polynomial commitment scheme for our ring ... Wahby, “Brakedown: Linear-Time and Field-Agnostic SNARKs for R1CS,” in Advances in \nCryptology – CRYPTO 2023\nPolynomial Commitments\nWe need a  (multivariate) polynomial commitment scheme for our ring\n●\nCCC+25 uses Brakedown [1]\n○\nLinear time prover\n○\nSquare-root time veriﬁer\n●\nOur commitments are O(N) smaller now, let’s use Basefold[2]:\n○\nQuasilinear prover\n○\nPolylog veriﬁer\n[1] A. Golovnev, J. Lee, S. Setty, J. Thaler, and R. S. Wahby, “Brakedown: Linear-Time and Field-Agnostic SNARKs for R1CS,” in Advances in \nCryptology – CRYPTO 2023\n[2] H. Zeilberger, B. Chen, and B. Fisch, “BaseFold: Eﬃcient Field-Agnostic Polynomial Commitment Schemes from Foldable Codes,” in \nAdvances in Cryptology – CRYPTO 2024--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBatched openings - JoltBook (https://jolt.a16zcrypto.com/how/optimizations/batched-openings.html)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; # Batched openings\n\nJolt batches polynomial evaluation claims to amortize the cost of its final opening proof. ... Consider the case of two polynomials, opened at two d